-- Sample data for a fresh deployment: the accounts, buildings and tickets
-- from local development. Loaded by POST /api/migrations/seed (db admin
-- only), which empties these tables first, so the ids below are kept.
--
-- Generated with:
--   pg_dump --data-only --column-inserts -t users -t buildings -t building_floors
--           -t locations -t incidents -t messages -t ticket_reads
-- with the session SET lines removed. Column names are spelled out because
-- a table's column order differs between a fresh database and one that was
-- upgraded in steps. Branches are not included: the migration seeds them
-- with the same ids (1 = Princeton-Plainsboro, 2 = Miami).
--
-- Edited after the dump so the data looks like a working queue: 18 tickets
-- are assigned (a mix of assigned, in progress, blocked, resolved and closed)
-- and the other 71 are open and unassigned. The resolved and closed tickets
-- took anywhere from about an hour to six days, so the statistics page has
-- something to average. Every ticket carries its branch_id: 81 at
-- Princeton-Plainsboro (buildings A, B and C) and 8 at Miami (Violent
-- Crimes), so each branch's facility admin has their own queue.
--
-- Every ticket has a category (plumbing, electrical, hvac, ...; the list is
-- in backend/incidents/models/incident.py), chosen from its title, so the
-- "by category" statistic has a spread of bars. The two throwaway "test"
-- tickets are 'other'.
SELECT pg_catalog.setval('public.buildings_id_seq', 4, true);
SELECT pg_catalog.setval('public.incidents_id_seq', 82, true);
SELECT pg_catalog.setval('public.locations_id_seq', 76, true);
SELECT pg_catalog.setval('public.messages_id_seq', 118, true);
SELECT pg_catalog.setval('public.users_id_seq', 19, true);
